(a) Which activation function performed best, and why do you think so? Relate your
answer to your learning curves and to Q1.3.

The activation function that performed the best based on validation scores was ReLU. ReLU had the best result for validation macro-F1 (0.746), then tanh (0.736) and finally sigmoid (0.726). However, when verifying with test scores, sigmoid, tanh, and ReLU respectively had .696, .696, and .693 accuracy, which does not show a clear "winner." When looking at the learning curves, all three reach their minimum validation loss early and then stay stable or rise, which shows overfitting. ReLU has the largest gap between the validation and training loss. Sigmoid was the most susceptible to a small learning rate, which aligns with Q1.3's findings. The vanishing gradients idea from Q1.3 that shows that sigmoid's small max derivative slows down network learning isn't shown as much in this expeirment due to shallower networks, but to see a small example, with plain SGD and lr 0.001, sigmoid hardly learned (macro-F1 0.375 for [64, 32]) while tanh and ReLU reached about 0.69-0.72 wiht the same architecture.


(b) How did your optimizer change convergence speed and final performance compared
with plain SGD?

The optimizer that was chosen was momentum (β = 0.9). When it came to convergence speed, the momentum optimizer appears to be quicker to converge for smaller learning rates. At a learning rate of 0.001, momentum got to a higher validation macro-F1 than plain SGD after the same 100 epochs (0.717 vs. 0.570 for sigmoid [64]). For final performance, the momentum opimizer did not perform significantly better when compared to plain SGD. When looking at tanh [64, 32] at learning rate 0.01, for example, the momentum’s training macro-F1 was 0.924 vs. SGD’s 0.754, while the validation macro-F1 was lower: 0.680 versus 0.736. 


(c) Is there evidence of overfitting in any configuration? What would you do about it?

Yes, there is evidence of overfitting in a few configurations. When looking at the learning curves for ReLU and tanh specifically, it is clear that training loss keeps going down while validation loss has stopped improving. Some notable configurations to observe this is with {ReLU [64, 32], momentum, 0.01} that has a training accuracy of 97.9% and a validation accuracy of 66.1%, and {Tanh [64, 32], momentum, 0.01} with a training accuracy of 92.8% and a validation accuracy of 69.6%.To rectify this, I would implement early stopping at the epoch of minimum validation loss, or add regularization to counteract the overfitting.


(d) How does your best network compare with the logistic-regression baseline? What
does this say about your dataset?

The network that scored the bets based on validation results was {relu, [64, 32], momentum, 0.001} which had a 69.26% test accuracy and 0.687 macro-F1, while logistic regression scored 68.90% and 0.679. Since this is a very small difference, it can be said that the dataset pattern can be captured relatively well with the linear model. The additional structure and complexity of the neural network likely is not needed when considering the lack of significantly improved results. 